1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,249
Every dashboard starts with a question that someone has to translate into a tool.

2
00:00:06,249 --> 00:00:08,619
Semaphore skips the translation.

3
00:00:08,619 --> 00:00:18,173
You describe the page in plain English, you review what it drafts, and leadership asks questions that your reviewed work answers.

4
00:00:18,173 --> 00:00:20,543
All of it runs on this computer.

5
00:00:20,793 --> 00:00:26,050
Semaphore is a dashboard workbench for analysts, and a question box for leadership.

6
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:32,272
Three pieces: a launcher, a small server on this computer, and the web page.

7
00:00:32,272 --> 00:00:38,814
Double-click the launcher, and it starts the server, which hands your browser a one-time link.

8
00:00:39,583 --> 00:00:44,497
The server listens on loopback only, and keeps hoists, the catalog and the playbook as plain files in one home folder.

9
00:00:45,197 --> 00:00:49,373
Halyard feeds it backlog health as four CSV files, read in the browser.

10
00:00:49,373 --> 00:00:55,352
PacketRat and Eight Bells exports load the same way, tested so far on sample files.

11
00:00:56,052 --> 00:01:00,746
For drafting, the server calls Fairlead with its own key from Credential Manager.

12
00:01:00,746 --> 00:01:05,423
Fairlead routes to the model, and gets its model keys from Lazarette.

13
00:01:05,423 --> 00:01:07,392
The page never sees a key.

14
00:01:08,092 --> 00:01:13,620
Databases and the data lake use Semaphore's own connectors, and still need validation.

15
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:16,463
Routing them through Fairlead is planned.

16
00:01:17,074 --> 00:01:24,282
Safety: one read-only select per query, an analyst accepts every draft, and leadership sees reviewed answers only.

17
00:01:24,282 --> 00:01:25,696
Semaphore ships as one folder.

18
00:01:25,696 --> 00:01:32,642
Download the zip, unblock it, extract it, and double-click Launch-Semaphore.

19
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:35,039
Nothing to install, no admin rights.

20
00:01:35,739 --> 00:01:42,546
It starts a small server on this computer, on a loopback port, and gives your browser a one-time session link.

21
00:01:42,546 --> 00:01:44,806
Nothing leaves the machine.

22
00:01:45,506 --> 00:01:52,615
Four ways in: continue the example, a new page, your own data, or the leadership view.

23
00:01:52,865 --> 00:01:55,296
Models come through Fairlead.

24
00:01:55,296 --> 00:02:01,113
Its OpenAI key sits in Lazarette, the vault, and Fairlead only sees what you share.

25
00:02:01,813 --> 00:02:04,933
On Fairlead's Clients page, pick Semaphore and install.

26
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:09,949
The key lands in Windows Credential Manager, and the page never holds it.

27
00:02:10,649 --> 00:02:12,879
A config file picks the route.

28
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:17,127
This demo data is synthetic, so it uses the smart route.

29
00:02:17,127 --> 00:02:21,627
Remove these two lines and drafts stay on the local route, on this computer.

30
00:02:21,877 --> 00:02:24,393
This is a hoist: one page of flags.

31
00:02:24,393 --> 00:02:29,853
The brief across the top is plain English, and Semaphore highlights what it understood.

32
00:02:30,724 --> 00:02:32,842
Click Gulf and the whole page filters.

33
00:02:33,542 --> 00:02:35,559
The monthly trend ignores it on purpose.

34
00:02:36,259 --> 00:02:41,742
Flip a flag over to read what it shows in plain English, and the exact SQL behind the number.

35
00:02:42,358 --> 00:02:46,563
The AI switch is on, so drafts go through Fairlead.

36
00:02:46,563 --> 00:02:51,032
Describe a flag in plain English: top ten hulls by labor cost.

37
00:02:51,732 --> 00:02:56,457
A few seconds later: a table, a confidence figure, and a note that Fairlead drafted it.

38
00:02:56,457 --> 00:03:00,040
Every key the model used was checked against the catalog.

39
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:03,701
Accept it, and the playbook keeps it.

40
00:03:03,701 --> 00:03:06,880
Brief me reads the page back, every sentence tied to a flag.

41
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:11,359
Halyard writes backlog health for Semaphore as four CSV files.

42
00:03:12,059 --> 00:03:14,391
This is the real export from the Halyard demo.

43
00:03:15,091 --> 00:03:16,910
Add them under Data.

44
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:24,092
Semaphore recognises Halyard's schema and proposes measures and fields: thirty-four measures, sixty-three fields.

45
00:03:24,792 --> 00:03:25,977
Now describe flags.

46
00:03:25,977 --> 00:03:29,428
Items needing review: seven.

47
00:03:29,428 --> 00:03:34,519
Items by type: twenty stories, seven tasks, four features.

48
00:03:34,519 --> 00:03:35,319
Items by state.

49
00:03:35,297 --> 00:03:39,968
Features by status: three on track, one at risk.

50
00:03:39,968 --> 00:03:43,187
Sprint velocity, planned against done.

51
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:45,749
A person accepts each draft.

52
00:03:45,749 --> 00:03:49,176
Accepted flags are what leadership can ask about.

53
00:03:49,876 --> 00:03:55,656
In the Ask view, the same numbers answer questions, labelled as reviewed.

54
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:59,420
The decision: can we commit the next sprint?

55
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:06,331
Four numbers decide it: items needing review, ready stories, blockers, and features at risk.

56
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:09,319
First, the raw export in PowerShell.

57
00:04:09,319 --> 00:04:13,148
Four commands, typed at a steady pace: about eleven seconds.

58
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:22,056
Now Semaphore, same four questions, same typing speed.

59
00:04:22,056 --> 00:04:28,258
One import, four plain-English briefs, four accepts: about twenty seconds.

60
00:04:28,258 --> 00:04:31,269
For one analyst and four numbers, PowerShell is faster.

61
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:48,522
But now a leader can ask the question themselves, and the answer comes back in under a second, labelled reviewed.

62
00:04:49,222 --> 00:04:51,521
The answer: not yet.

63
00:04:51,521 --> 00:04:57,627
Nothing is ready, seven items need review, there is one blocker and one feature at risk.

64
00:04:57,627 --> 00:05:00,443
Sample data: the Halyard demo backlog.

65
00:05:00,443 --> 00:05:02,975
Semaphore reads more than CSV.

66
00:05:02,975 --> 00:05:08,464
Here is what connects, and how well each one is proven.

67
00:05:08,899 --> 00:05:16,326
In the browser: CSV, Halyard exports, PacketRat and Eight Bells exports, and Qlik load scripts.

68
00:05:16,326 --> 00:05:21,178
A QVD is Qlik's own format, so export it to CSV first.

69
00:05:21,498 --> 00:05:28,803
Through the server: databases over ODBC or OLE DB, including Postgres on AWS RDS, Oracle, SQL Server and Access.

70
00:05:28,803 --> 00:05:32,539
Plus an S3 data lake through Athena, with a cost guard.

71
00:05:32,539 --> 00:05:36,502
The AWS paths are built and tested against simulated AWS.

72
00:05:36,502 --> 00:05:38,062
Live validation is pending.

73
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:40,509
Here is a real database.

74
00:05:40,509 --> 00:05:43,700
This Access file holds the Halyard backlog.

75
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:47,978
One entry in sources.json, then pick the table, profile and add.

76
00:05:48,563 --> 00:05:57,345
Now the same plain-English brief runs a real query through the server, and the SQL is shown.

77
00:05:57,595 --> 00:06:05,798
Share as a report saves one HTML file with working filters, summarised numbers only, optionally locked with a passphrase.

78
00:06:06,498 --> 00:06:12,754
It also reads PacketRat and Eight Bells exports, tested so far on synthetic files.

79
00:06:13,254 --> 00:06:14,854
Semaphore drafts.

80
00:06:14,854 --> 00:06:16,171
People review.

81
00:06:16,171 --> 00:06:17,677
Leadership asks.

82
00:06:17,677 --> 00:06:26,897
And a thought for your team: before you rent a dashboard tool, ask what you could build in a week.

83
00:06:26,897 --> 00:06:30,943
The new frontier is compute and creativity.

84
00:06:30,943 --> 00:06:32,543
That's the watch.

85
00:06:32,543 --> 00:06:33,578
Fair winds.
